Title: ZODIAC WARS: THE RISE OF ARIES
Credit: Draft 1 — PROPOSED, not approved
Author: Prepared for Ω SYD OMEGA 91717 STUDIOS GLOBAL under the founder's direction
Draft date: 2026-11-07
Notes: ACT ONE of three. Saga chapter 1 (THE ORIGIN). Characters other than Kael Marren, Adaeze Okonkwo-Reyes and Vahn Ossara are proposed inventions.

FADE IN:

EXT. THE SOLAR SYSTEM — NIGHT

Black. Twelve worlds hang in a slow ring around a pale sun, each lit in its own colour: Mars rust, Venus gold, Neptune deep blue, and nine more. Between them, nothing. A silence so old it has a texture.

SUPER: "TWELVE THOUSAND YEARS AFTER THE ACCORD."

Under the silence, so low we feel it in the seats before we hear it: a HUM. One note. It does not belong to any instrument.

CUT TO:

INT. THE ARES FOUNDRY, OLYMPUS RIDGE — MARS — NIGHT

A cathedral of heat. A vault four storeys high, lit by a river of molten bronze running down a channel in the floor toward a mould the size of a house: a BELL, upside down in its pit, waiting.

Forty APPRENTICES and JOURNEYMEN stand on the gantries in heat-cloth, faces red. On the lowest gantry, at the lip of the pit: MASTER ORLA TEVANE, sixties, forearms a map of old burns, her left hand a plain steel prosthetic she has never bothered to hide.

Beside her, the youngest apprentice on the floor: KAEL MARREN, nineteen, thin, a mark of soot across his jaw, eyes that keep counting things. He is holding a long iron rod and not looking at the bronze. He is looking at the people under the gantry.

ORLA
(not loud, carrying)
The Foundry's oath. Who says the cost?

A beat. The whole floor waits. Someone coughs.

KAEL
I will, Master.

ORLA
Say it so the one at the back hears.

KAEL
(louder; he has done this before and still hates it)
Forty tonnes of Olympus bronze. Six weeks of the cooling crew's sleep. Three hundred hours of the Ridge's power, which is the Ridge's winter. And if the mould fails —
(he looks down)
— the eleven people standing under the east gantry when it does.

Murmurs. Someone laughs, not kindly. A broad journeyman, DRAVEN, calls down:

DRAVEN
Every pour, Marren. Every pour you tell us we might die.

KAEL
Every pour you might.

ORLA
(to the floor)
We say it so that when we pour, we pour on purpose. Move the east crew back six paces.

Grumbling, but they move. Orla watches Kael, unsmiling. It is the closest she comes to praise.

ORLA (CONT'D)
Pour.

She drops her steel hand. The sluice opens. Light.

The bronze goes down the channel like a held breath released, into the mould, and the foundry fills with a sound that is part roar, part chord.

Kael steps to the rail. His rod trembles very slightly in his hand. He frowns.

KAEL
Master. Do you hear that?

ORLA
I hear forty tonnes of metal.

KAEL
Underneath.

He crouches, presses his palm flat against the iron deck. The vibration is not the pour. It is slower. A pulse, perfectly even, like something enormous breathing in its sleep.

He pulls a small TUNING FORK from his belt, strikes it on his knee, holds it to the deck. The fork's tone climbs, bends, and locks to the hum. A brass dial on his belt kit ticks to a reading. He reads it twice.

KAEL (CONT'D)
Nine point one seven one seven.

ORLA
There is no such note on the Accord tables.

KAEL
I know. I checked.

ORLA
(a beat; she crouches beside him, steel hand on the deck)
When?

KAEL
Since the cold pour in spring. It's been getting louder.

Orla says nothing for a long moment. Then, quietly:

ORLA
Tell no one.

KAEL
Why?

ORLA
Because I don't know yet whether it's a gift or a fault, and the first people to hear it will decide for us.

INT. THE ARES FOUNDRY — LATER

The pour is finished. The crews filter out through the heat-locks, joking in the loose way of people who have not died. Kael walks the floor alone, ticking items off a slate: sluices, moulds, the cooling rig. He is the last to leave, always.

At the far wall, under an iron arch, stands a plinth carrying a squat dark object wrapped in oilcloth. Kael unwraps it. It is a TONGUE OF BRONZE, the forearm-long clapper that will, when the bell is hung, make it speak. Green with age at the grip, bright at the strike-end. Cast by Orla's own hands.

He weighs it. He is not supposed to touch it.

ORLA (O.S.)
It is heavier than it looks.

She is in the arch. She has been there a while.

ORLA (CONT'D)
That is the Aries tongue. When the Accord bell is hung in the Hall of the Seats on Earth, that is what rings it. Twelve Houses, twelve tongues, one chime every twelve years, to remind them they swore.

KAEL
Why does it weigh so much?

ORLA
Because it's the only one of the twelve that's allowed to be struck in anger. The rest have to be asked.

KAEL
(small smile)
That's a joke.

ORLA
That is the Aries joke. We tell it in two parts. The second is that it has never been struck in anger.

She takes it from him, rewraps it with the care of someone dressing a wound.

ORLA (CONT'D)
It ships to Earth at the end of the week. You will go with it as my second.

KAEL
(stunned)
Master, I'm nineteen.

ORLA
I was nineteen.

KAEL
You're sixty.

ORLA
I was nineteen once. Don't push it.

She almost smiles. Then every furnace in the foundry ROARS at once.

INT. THE ARES FOUNDRY — CONTINUOUS

The fires jump. Flame bows over the sluices as if something enormous has inhaled. The bell in its pit begins to HUM, loudly: the note Kael measured, the Accord bell sounding unstruck.

Orla grabs Kael's collar and pulls him under the arch as dust rains from the vault.

Through the foundry's high slit windows: the night sky over Mars TEARS. A seam of pale light opens from horizon to horizon like a lid lifting, and where it passes, the stars rearrange themselves, as if the night were being unfolded.

Workers stream back in, shouting. Draven, white-faced.

DRAVEN
Every bell on the Ridge is ringing. The tower bells, the quarry bells. Nobody's touching them.

A VOICE from the foundry's walls. Calm, level, a perimeter system's voice with something almost like pride.

SENTINEL (V.O.)
I am Sentinel. Your perimeter is mine to hold.

(beat)

SENTINEL (V.O.) (CONT'D)
All twelve Accord bells are sounding. This has not happened since the Accord. Please remain inside the walls.

Kael looks at Orla. She is staring at the ceiling as though she can see through stone.

ORLA
(barely audible)
Twelve thousand years.

KAEL
Master?

ORLA
(turning, hard)
Say the cost, Kael.

KAEL
Of what?

ORLA
Of whatever comes through that door.

She nods at the main foundry gate. It is quiet. It will not be for long.

CUT TO:

INT. THE BALANCE COURT, MERIDIAN CITY — EARTH — DAY

A different light entirely. Pale stone, tall windows, ordinary morning. A long chamber where a hearing is under way and nobody is shouting, which is the point.

At the head of the table: ADAEZE OKONKWO-REYES, thirty-four, in the grey sash of a mediator of the Balance Court, dark hair braided back, a notebook open in front of her filled with margin notes in four colours. Her face is calm in the way of someone who has chosen calm every day for years and now owns it.

Across from her, two delegations. On the left, a MARS FOREMAN in a heat-stained jacket. On the right, a NEPTUNIAN ARCHIVE-WARDEN in a drifting grey robe, fingertips stained blue.

ADAEZE
We are agreed that the ice lease is Neptune's by the Accord, and the pumping rights are Mars's by the Accord, and that these two facts are in conflict.

FOREMAN
We have been saying that for forty years.

ARCHIVE-WARDEN
We have been saying we are agreed for forty years. You keep saying it louder.

ADAEZE
(a flicker of dry humour)
This is the first time I've seen you both disagree about the same thing.

She flips a page, finds the clause, reads it aloud.

ADAEZE (CONT'D)
Clause nine. "Water bound to one House may be drawn by another only with the consent of the one who holds it." That's the letter.
(she looks up)
The reason is that twelve thousand years ago somebody died of thirst on a world that had water they weren't allowed to touch.

A pause. The Foreman and Warden look, for a moment, less like adversaries.

ADAEZE (CONT'D)
So. Who in this room is thirsty?

Neither answers. Then, from all across the city, a BELL begins to ring.

Not the Court's bell. Every bell. Cathedral bells, school bells, the little brass hand-bell on the table beside her notebook, trembling with no one touching it. The windows hum.

The Foreman and Warden are on their feet, shouting over each other. Adaeze doesn't move. She is listening.

ADAEZE (CONT'D)
(to herself)
No. That's not a fault in the tower. That's a note.

CLERK (O.S.)
Mediator Okonkwo-Reyes!

A young CLERK bursts in, panting, a ring of keys at his belt.

CLERK (CONT'D)
The Hall of the Seats. The doors. They've opened.

ADAEZE
Which doors?

CLERK
(a swallow)
All of them. The sealed ones.

INT. THE HALL OF THE SEATS, BALANCE COURT — CONTINUOUS

A vast circular hall under a glass dome, twelve great stone chairs set in a ring, each carved with the sign and symbol of a House: Ram, Bull, Twins, Crab, and the rest. Dust-sheeted, silent, used only for ceremony.

And in the middle of the ring, where the floor has always been bare: a thirteenth chair, plain, unadorned, pale. It stands now in a column of light that falls from the dome, though the sky is overcast.

Adaeze steps over the threshold slowly. The Clerk hangs back at the door.

She circles the thirteenth chair. Her hand drifts out toward its arm. Stops an inch short.

ADAEZE
(whisper)
It's warm.

CLERK
(O.S.)
Mediator, the Accord says nobody sits in it.

ADAEZE
The Accord says nobody sits in it unbidden.

She straightens, hand withdrawn, watching the empty seat.

ADAEZE (CONT'D)
Seal the Hall. Nobody goes in. Nobody talks about this until I've read the whole text.

CLERK
There's a hundred thousand words of it.

ADAEZE
Then I'll be a while.

She looks at the thirteenth chair one more time. The hum in the room finds a pitch and sits there, steady, waiting.

CUT TO:

INT. THE DEEP ARCHIVE, NEPTUNE — NIGHT

A cathedral of water. Glass tanks the height of towers rise through a blue-lit hall, each containing slow-drifting scrolls, glass plates, bones, tiny glowing things nobody has named. A web of walkways crosses the dark.

On a lantern-lit platform at the heart of it: SOLENNE VARR, seventy-three, an archivist in grey, with a fisherman's hands and an almost indecent amount of patience. Beside her, wrapped in a blanket, sits IMA, eight, large-eyed, supposed to be asleep.

SOLENNE
This is the last time. Then bed.

IMA
You said that last time.

SOLENNE
Last time I was lying.

Ima grins. Solenne settles, the way storytellers do, before a lamp.

SOLENNE (CONT'D)
Before there were twelve, there were four. They did not have names, because they did not need to be told apart from the world. One held what is carried across a death. One held the distance that lets two things be separate. One held the room that lets anything be made. And one held the whole of it, so none of the others had to.

IMA
That's the All.

SOLENNE
That's the All. And then twelve came, and took the fire and the water and the wind and the metal and the sand, and divided the worlds, and swore a peace. And the four were not invited.

IMA
Why?

SOLENNE
Because the twelve couldn't hold a throne and a memory at the same time, and they chose the throne.

IMA
Who won?

SOLENNE
Won what, little tide?

IMA
The war. Before the peace. Who won?

Solenne holds the child's gaze for a moment.

SOLENNE
Nobody.

IMA
Somebody has to win a war.

SOLENNE
(softly)
Then nobody ended it. It was only paused.

In the tanks behind them the water RISES. Not violently: a slow lifting of the whole column, as if the sea outside the Archive were leaning in. The lamplight shivers on the glass.

IMA
Grandmother, it's doing it again.

SOLENNE
(already moving, calm)
Yes. Don't be afraid.

She stands, takes the child's hand, and leads her to the rail. In the largest tank, the water has formed a face: not a human face, a tide's idea of one. Vast, slow, listening.

POSEIDON (V.O.)
(many voices of water, unhurried)
Remember.

SOLENNE
(low)
I am trying to, my lord.

POSEIDON (V.O.)
Remember faster.

Ima, wide-eyed, whispers:

IMA
It's the same note.

SOLENNE
What note?

IMA
The one in my ears. It's been there since this morning.

Solenne looks at her, at the water, at the glass. For the first time she looks her age.

SOLENNE
(to herself)
So it is going to be me.

CUT TO:

EXT. THE ARES FOUNDRY, OLYMPUS RIDGE — MARS — NIGHT

Black ships slide over the Ridge, silent, no running lights. Matte, ribbed, shaped like blades with the edges ground off. They land on the foundry apron one after another without a sound.

The Foundry's perimeter lights hit them and flicker. Sentinel's voice, steady:

SENTINEL (V.O.)
Unregistered craft. State your House and your purpose.

No reply. A ramp falls from the first ship. A column of SOLDIERS in dull grey armour with no insignia marches down it. Their visors are blank.

Behind them, in a long coat the colour of old blood, steps REGENT VAHN OSSARA, forty-five, hair cropped close, a face that was once handsome and is now something more useful. His eyes are very calm. He walks like a man who has already decided how this ends.

SENTINEL (V.O.) (CONT'D)
You are inside the Ridge perimeter. This is your last request.

OSSARA
(pleasantly, to the air)
Hello, Sentinel. You've aged well.

SENTINEL (V.O.)
I do not recognise you.

OSSARA
No. You wouldn't. You were never told I existed.

He raises one gloved hand. The Foundry's lights go out, all at once.

INT. THE ARES FOUNDRY — NIGHT

Darkness, then emergency red. Kael and Orla stand with a handful of apprentices as the main gate shudders. Draven has a hammer. A girl, TAMSIN OKORO, eighteen, hair tied under a cooling cap, clings to Kael's arm.

ORLA
Behind the pit. All of you. Go.

TAMSIN
Master —

ORLA
Tamsin. Behind the pit.

They go, but Kael doesn't. Orla looks at him.

ORLA (CONT'D)
That's an order.

KAEL
I can count. I can tell you how many.

ORLA
How many?

KAEL
Thirty at the gate. Eight inside already. We're forty. And you're sixty and I'm not sure I can hit a man.

ORLA
(not unkind)
You can't. Get behind the pit.

The gate falls inward. Grey soldiers pour in, weapons level. Ossara strolls in behind them, hands loose, eyes sweeping the foundry the way a man walks through a house he used to live in.

He stops at the edge of the pit, looks down at the cooling bell, and smiles.

OSSARA
You kept it.

ORLA
(walking out into the light, steel hand clenched)
Hello, Vahn.

OSSARA
Master.

Kael, crouched behind the pit wall with Tamsin, goes very still.

ORLA
You were told not to come back.

OSSARA
I was told lots of things. I was told to say the cost before every pour. I did it for six years in this room.
(his smile thins)
I said it once for forty thousand people, and found out it was only a number. And numbers, Orla, can be paid.

ORLA
By whom?

OSSARA
By anyone willing to stop counting.

He walks to the plinth under the iron arch. The oilcloth is still half-off the bronze tongue. He reaches out. Orla puts her steel hand on his wrist. The soldiers move. Ossara raises a finger and they stop.

OSSARA (CONT'D)
The Nexus is awake. You can hear it. Every House that still has a bell is hearing it right now, and one of them is going to ring it, and I would rather it were somebody who knows what it's for.

ORLA
What is it for?

OSSARA
Ending the argument.

ORLA
(quietly)
It's for asking.

OSSARA
(almost kindly)
You always taught that they were the same thing.

He takes the tongue from the plinth. It is heavy, but he handles it as if it weighs nothing.

ORLA
Put it down.

OSSARA
No.

ORLA
Vahn —

He does not hit her. He does not speak. He simply turns the bronze tongue in his hand, strikes once, hard, across her temple, and she goes down.

The foundry does not breathe. Behind the pit, Tamsin's hand flies to her mouth; Kael clamps his over hers. His eyes are enormous.

Ossara looks down at Orla for a moment. Not triumph. Something worse: nothing.

OSSARA
(to no one)
A war is only expensive if you keep the books.

BEHIND HIM, in the red light, something stands.

It is taller than any man, lean, draped in the same blood-dark colour as his coat but without a coat. A figure of absence: a shape cut from the air, edged in ember. And all along its arms and chest are WOUNDS, dozens of them, open, deep, and not one drips.

THE UNBLED. It does not move. It watches Ossara like a hand that has finally found its glove.

From behind the pit, Kael sees it. Sees the wounds. Sees that the air near it shimmers with a faint heat. He knows what he is looking at without having ever been told.

KAEL
(barely a breath)
Aries.

Tamsin shakes her head, not understanding. Kael can't stop looking.

The Unbled turns its head, very slightly, toward the pit.

KAEL (CONT'D)
(a whisper; the instinct of a boy who has said it a thousand times)
Don't. Count it. Count it.

He pulls Tamsin down into the shadow just as the soldiers sweep the pit with a light.

INT. THE ARES FOUNDRY — LATER

Smoke. The grey soldiers are loading crates of bronze ingots onto sleds. Ossara supervises from the plinth, the tongue wrapped in cloth under his arm.

At the pit's edge, Orla lies where she fell. A soldier checks her pulse, shakes his head.

In the casting pit, ten feet below, Kael lies on his back in cooling slag, silent, holding Tamsin. His eyes are open. Tears run down into the soot.

Above them, boots. A flashlight swings across the pit wall, then away.

OSSARA (O.S.)
Leave the apprentices. We've no quarrel with children.

SOLDIER (O.S.)
And the bell?

OSSARA (O.S.)
Let it cool. It's not the one we need.

Boots recede. Engines whine up, one by one, then fade.

Silence. A long silence. Then, so quietly, Tamsin:

TAMSIN
He killed her.

KAEL
(very flat)
He took the tongue.

TAMSIN
He killed her, Kael.

KAEL
(sitting up, lifting a hand to wipe his face, and finding it won't stop shaking)
I know.

He gets to his feet. Climbs the pit ladder with deliberate care. Crosses to the body. Kneels. Closes Orla's eyes with one thumb, then the other.

He is about to speak when the foundry fire BLAZES behind him, white and gold.

INT. THE ARES FOUNDRY, THE FURNACE HEART — CONTINUOUS

The great central furnace, a cavern of coals the size of a ship, has lit itself. A figure of flame rises from it: not the Unbled's shape of absence, but its opposite, a heat with a face. Enormous, broad-shouldered, swirling.

ARES (V.O.)
(a voice like a forge opening)
Boy.

Kael does not turn. His hand is still on Orla's brow.

ARES (V.O.) (CONT'D)
They have taken the tongue to the Nexus. They will ring it. You have an hour before the first House answers.

KAEL
(not turning)
She's dead.

ARES (V.O.)
Yes.

KAEL
You're Ares.

ARES (V.O.)
I am the one this world was pressed toward. I am initiative. I am the first move. You are the first apprentice who has ever refused to flinch at the sight of my shadow. That is why I want you.

Kael stands. Turns. The figure of flame has descended, now at the edge of the coals.

KAEL
You didn't stop him.

ARES (V.O.)
It is not what I am.

KAEL
Then what are you for?

ARES (V.O.)
For this. For the instant after the world falls silent and someone decides to be the first to move.

Silence. Kael looks at Orla's body.

KAEL
If I go, the dead are unburied. The apprentices are alone. Tamsin has no one. The tongue is on a black ship that will be at the Nexus before I have a pilot.

ARES (V.O.)
You count too much.

KAEL
She told me it's the only thing that stops a pour from killing the people standing under it.

A long, heavy pause. The flames shift. Something like irritation, something like grudging respect.

ARES (V.O.)
She was right. I hate it.

(beat)

ARES (V.O.) (CONT'D)
I have been pressing Mars toward initiative for twelve thousand years and the one boy who truly understands what a first move costs is the one I cannot burn.

KAEL
Is that a compliment?

ARES (V.O.)
It is a problem. I will use it.

A hand of flame reaches toward Kael's forearm. He flinches. The fire touches. A mark BURNS in his skin, a ram's horns, curling, bright as a coal, then settling to a faint red line under the soot.

ARES (V.O.) (CONT'D)
Go to Earth. The thirteenth seat has opened. Reach it first.

KAEL
And if I get there and don't know what to do?

ARES (V.O.)
Say the cost.

(beat)

ARES (V.O.) (CONT'D)
It is the one thing the Unbled cannot hear.

The flame collapses back into coals. The furnace goes quiet.

Kael stands in the dark with the mark glowing on his arm.

EXT. OLYMPUS RIDGE LANDING APRON — DAWN

A battered cargo hauler squats on the apron. Draven and two other journeymen load a crate of rations. Tamsin stands beside Kael with a pack too big for her.

TAMSIN
I'm coming.

KAEL
You're staying.

TAMSIN
Someone has to bury her.

KAEL
Exactly.

Tamsin glares. Then, very quietly:

TAMSIN
She'd have wanted you to say the words.

Kael looks down at the apron. Up at the pale seam still glowing across the sky.

KAEL
(clearly, to the whole Ridge, not loudly)
Orla Tevane. Master of the Ares Foundry. Eleven thousand pours. One life taken when a man stopped counting. The cost was her.

(a beat; his voice cracks)

KAEL (CONT'D)
I'll pay what's owed.

The journeymen bow their heads. Draven, gruff, puts a hand on Kael's shoulder.

DRAVEN
Go.

Kael climbs into the hauler. Tamsin raises a hand. He raises his, mark glowing red against the dawn.

SENTINEL (V.O.)
I am Sentinel. I will ride this one with you. The perimeter is the ship.

KAEL
(small, wet laugh)
Of course it is.

The hauler lifts. Behind it, Olympus Ridge shrinks. Ahead, the rift in the sky pulls wider.

SUPER: "TWO DAYS TO EARTH."

SENTINEL (V.O.)
Black ships have broken orbit. They are ahead of us.

KAEL
How far?

SENTINEL (V.O.)
Six hours.

KAEL
What can we do?

SENTINEL (V.O.)
Burn everything we have.

Kael looks at the dial of the engine, the drum of fuel, the small mark glowing on his arm.

KAEL
Say the cost.

SENTINEL (V.O.)
(a beat)
All of it.

KAEL
(pushing the throttle forward)
Then pay it.

The hauler's engines flare white. The ship vanishes into the seam of light.

END OF ACT ONE
